Information Criteria (ICs), like the AIC change for each model, the lower value indicates better fit.

ICs are commonly use to compare non-nested models, the big disadvantage is that there is no clear guidelines of how big of a differences its relevant and no p-value to test the null hypothesis of equal fit between models.

Another option, you can use the nonnest2 package, that uses the Voung method to compare non-nested models, it works with lavaan objects

Preacher, K. J., & Merkle, E. C. (2012). The problem of model selection uncertainty in structural equation modeling. Psychological Methods, 17(1), 1–14. https://doi.org/10.1037/a0026804
Merkle, E. C., You, D., & Preacher, K. J. (2016). Testing nonnested structural equation models. Psychological Methods, 21(2), 151–163. https://doi.org/10.1037/met0000038

